---
/**
 * The support page.
 *
 * It exists because the app links to it: `SupportApp` in swift-support-kit renders
 * Help items for the tracker, the feedback form and `siteURL + supportPath`, and
 * that last one lands here.
 *
 * It routes rather than explains. Three channels, and the only thing a reader has
 * to get right is which one their problem belongs in. The order differs from
 * cupertino's on purpose: an Armada report usually has to quote a session title or
 * a project path, which is why `preferIssueTracker` is false in
 * apps/apple/Armada/Support.swift, so the private form leads and the shared public
 * tracker comes second.
 */
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import Nav from "../components/Nav.astro";
import {
  APP_NAME,
  FEEDBACK_URL,
  ISSUES_URL,
  PRICING,
  SELLING,
  SITE_URL,
  SUPPORT_EMAIL,
  SUPPORT_EMAIL_HREF,
} from "../config";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "ContactPage",
  name: "Support",
  url: `${SITE_URL}/support/`,
  isPartOf: { "@id": `${SITE_URL}/#website` },
  publisher: { "@id": `${SITE_URL}/#organization` },
};

const h2 = "m-0 font-display text-[22px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em]";
const lead = "mt-5 mb-0 max-w-[62ch] text-[15px] leading-[1.65] text-fg-dim text-pretty";
const prose = "mt-4 mb-0 max-w-[62ch] text-[15px] leading-[1.65] text-fg-dim text-pretty";
const link = "underline underline-offset-2";
const strong = "font-medium text-fg";
---

<Layout
  title={`Support · ${APP_NAME}`}
  description={`Get help with ${APP_NAME}: a private feedback form for reports that quote your own sessions, the public tracker, or email for licences.`}
  schema={schema}
>
  <Nav />
  <main id="main" tabindex="-1" class="wrap py-20">
    <div class="mono text-accent">Support</div>
    <h1 class="mt-3 mb-0 font-display text-[clamp(28px,4.4vw,40px)] font-semibold tracking-[-0.04em]">
      Support
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-4 mb-0 max-w-[62ch] text-[16px] leading-[1.6] text-fg-dim text-pretty">
      Three ways in, and which one to use comes down to a single question:{" "}
      <strong class={strong}>does telling us what went wrong mean quoting your own work?</strong>
    </p>
    {
      !SELLING && (
        <p class="mt-4 mb-0 max-w-[62ch] text-[15px] leading-[1.6] text-fg-muted text-pretty">
          {APP_NAME} is not released yet, so there are no licences to help with. Questions before
          launch are welcome through any of the routes below.
        </p>
      )
    }

    <section class="mt-14">
      <h2 class={h2}>Anything that quotes your own work</h2>
      <p class={lead}>
        A useful {APP_NAME} report often has to include a session title, a project path or an account
        folder name. The <a href={FEEDBACK_URL} class={link}>feedback form</a> is private: no account
        is needed, and the email address is optional. Leave it blank and the report is anonymous.
      </p>
      <p class={prose}>
        <strong class={strong}>Choosing Send Feedback in the app does not send anything.</strong> It
        hands your browser a URL and stops. Everything that URL carries (the app version, the macOS
        version, the Mac model and the language) is visible in the address bar, and on the page as four
        fields you can edit or clear before pressing Send.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14">
      <h2 class={h2}>A bug or an idea you are happy to share</h2>
      <p class={lead}>
        The <a href={ISSUES_URL} rel="noopener" class={link}>public tracker</a> is shared by every
        Magenta Creations app. It suits anything that is not about your own sessions: a feature
        request, a question about how a figure is read, a pane that looks wrong. It is public, so the
        answer is there for whoever asks next.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14">
      <h2 class={h2}>Licences and payment</h2>
      <p class={lead}>
        Email <a href={SUPPORT_EMAIL_HREF} class={link}>{SUPPORT_EMAIL}</a> for a key that will not
        activate, a key you have lost, or a refund within the {PRICING.refundDays}-day window, no reason
        needed. A key is checked on your Mac and never sent anywhere, so a reply is the only way we can
        look at one: quote the address you bought with rather than the key itself.
      </p>
      <p class={prose}>
        What a key covers, and what the trial does, is set out in the{" "}
        <a href="/terms" class={link}>licence terms</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14">
      <h2 class={h2}>Finding the Help pane</h2>
      <p class={lead}>
        {APP_NAME} lives in the menu bar and has no Dock icon. Open its settings, and the Help pane
        links here and to the feedback form. Opening either one directly in a browser works exactly as
        well: the app only ever hands them over.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14">
      <h2 class={h2}>What to expect</h2>
      <p class={lead}>
        Everything gets read, and most things get an answer within a few working days. A report that
        says which version, which macOS, and whether the session was Claude Code or Codex can usually
        be answered in the first reply rather than the third.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
